WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
 Hello and welcome to my video that shows how to get started and install Python on Microsoft Windows.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.000
 Okay, so it's not too hard. We're going to both install Python 3 and we're going to install Text Editor.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.000
 And so I'm just going to go into Google and I'm going to install Python 3.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.000
 And my top link is downloading Python. And there is my link for downloading Python 3.5.2.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
 This version of my class uses Python 3. I have an earlier class that you may have seen that uses Python 2.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
 But in this class, we're going to do this. Now it might take you a while to download this.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
 I've actually already downloaded it. Now the other thing we need is a programmer text editor.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.000
 And you can really use any programmer text editor. We've used Notepad Plus in the past.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:02.000
 We've used J-Edit in the past. I like Atom, Atom.io, T-O-M dot I-O.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.000
 Mostly because it works the same on Windows and Mac and Linux.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:16.000
 But you can really use any text editor that you like. Just don't use Word or TextEdit that comes with the operating system.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
 You need a programming editor that doesn't mess with weird characters or weird lines or strange formats.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.000
 You must have a real programmer editor. So I've already downloaded this as well.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
 And so I won't waste the time waiting to download it. But let's go ahead and do the installation.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:50.000
 So these things ended up in my downloads file. So I'm going to Downloads.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
 And I'll start installing Python 3.5.2. Now it's going to ask me some things.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
 Add Python 3.5 to the path. That's a good idea. Install the launcher for all users.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:14.000
 I'm going to add that. Maybe you will. Maybe you won't do that. It's going to tell me where it's going to install it.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
 Install now. Of course it's going to ask me for permission to do these things.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
 And now it's running through the installation.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
 Okay. So there we go. You could maybe click on this online tutorial and documentation.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:42.000
 But we're just going to close this. And I'm going to start and run the Windows command line.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:51.000
 Now you may have all kinds of fancy ways to run Python, but I like running the command line.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:03.000
 C-O-M-M-A-N-D. I like running the command line because after a while it's important to know what folder things are being run in.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
 And so here's this command line. And I should be able to type Python here.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:23.000
 And so now I'm in Python 3.2. And the chevron prompt here is the Python interpreter where it's asking for Python commands and I can say print.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.000
 Hello world. Of course this is what we tend to print all the time.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:39.000
 But I can make a mistake. I can say.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.000
 Right. And it'll complain to me.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
 Now to get out of this I can either type control Z or quit. In this case I'm going to type control Z.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.000
 And I'm back to the prompt. A couple of things.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
 I can do a dir to see what folders and files I have. And that is like my desktop.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
 And then the CD command tells me where I'm at in the folder.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
 That means I'm in the user's directory. Dr. Chuck.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.000
 So I have now installed Python. I ran the Python interpreter to verify it.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
 I said print hello world.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
 And so now what I'm going to do is I'm going to actually install Adam.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:37.000
 I already had this downloaded so let's go ahead and install Adam on my computer.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:47.000
 OK. So Adam is now installed and is kind of telling us what to do. I'm going to actually just close all these windows, close this window, close everything.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:54.000
 And I'm going to create a file. I'm going to say print in this case.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
 Let's see if I can make this bigger. I can make it bigger.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.000
 So I'm going to type print hello from a file. OK.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.000
 And I'm going to save this. I'm going to say file.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:23.000
 Save as. And what I'm going to do is I'm going to go to my desktop.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.000
 And I'm going to make a folder on the desktop.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.000
 I'm going to call this folder PY4E.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
 So I now have a folder on the desktop.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
 Move this here. I'll move this here.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.000
 And I'm going to go into PY4E.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:49.000
 And then I'm going to name this file first.py.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.000
 And you'll notice that when I save this.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.000
 When I save this, it syntax highlighted it.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
 That's one of the nice things about a programmer editor.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.000
 And so it says, oh, it's got a suffix of .py.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
 So therefore it knows that it's supposed to look pretty with Python

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.000
 and make this one color and make this another color.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.000
 The other thing that you'll notice is that I now have a folder called PY4E.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.000
 And if I'm in this command line, let me just start that up again.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.000
 I'll show you how to start the command line again.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
 Command.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.000
 Now, if I do a dir, I see the folders that I'm in.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
 And one of the folders that you can see here is the desktop folder.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.000
 So I'm going to say CD Desktop.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
 And then I'm going to type the dir command to see what folders are in the desktop.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
 These folders are the same as these folders.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.000
 These things are kind of virtual folders.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
 PY4E is PY4E.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:57.000
 Now I can type CD, which stands for Change Directory, PY4E.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.000
 And I can do a dir, and I see first.py.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
 And that's the same as if I'm diving into this folder.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.000
 Here's this file, first.py.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.000
 Windows hides the suffix, which is somewhat annoying and frustrating.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.000
 But that suffix is there, that file is there.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
 And so for me, one of the things you've got to figure out in Windows is how to

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.000
 make sure that you are in the same folder, users, Dr. Chuck, desktop, PY4E.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.000
 And that's this name in this file.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.000
 And here as well.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.000
 And now I'm going to run this program.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.000
 I'm going to type Python, first.py.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.000
 And you see that it ran the Python code, okay?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.000
 Another way you can do this is you can type first.py.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.000
 And that's because this file association has happened in Windows.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.000
 This doesn't work in Macintosh, this only works in Windows.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.000
 That all files with .py are expected to be Python, and

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.000
 it knows the Python interpreter where to run it, okay?

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.000
 And so I've got Python 3.0 installed, and that gets me started.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:13.000
 And so I hope that this little introduction about getting things started and

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.000
 writing your first Python program has been helpful to you.

